    <exercise number="63" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Weierstrass Substitution The German mathematician Karl Weierstrass (1815–1897) noticed that the substitution $$t = \tan\left( {x/2} \right)$$ will convert any rational function of $$\sin x$$ and $$\cos x$$ into an ordinary rational function of $$t$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>If $$t = \tan\left( {x/2} \right)$$ , $$- \pi &lt; x &lt; \pi$$ , sketch a right triangle or use trigonometric identities to show that $$\cos\frac{x}{2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}}$$ and $$\sin\frac{x}{2} = \frac{t}{\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}}$$</item>
          <item>Show that $$\cos x = \frac{1 - t^{2}}{1 + t^{2}}$$ and $$\sin x = \frac{2t}{1 + t^{2}}$$</item>
          <item>Show that $$dx = \frac{2}{1 + t^{2}}dt$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
